{"cuisine/french-guianan-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/french-guianan-cuisine","n":"French Guianan cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in France","intro":[["French Guianan cuisine or Guianan cuisine is a mixture of Creole, Bushinengue, and indigenous cuisines, supplemented by influences from the cuisines of more recent immigrant groups. Common ingredients include cassava, smoked fish, and smoked chicken. Creole restaurants may be found alongside Chinese restaurants in major cities such as Cayenne, Kourou and Saint-Laurent-du-Maroni."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"french-guianan-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_Guianan_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]}],"cont":"European cuisine"},"cuisine/shandong-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/shandong-cuisine","n":"Shandong cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in China","intro":[["Shandong cuisine, more commonly known in Chinese as Lu cuisine (魯菜), is one of the Eight Culinary Traditions of Chinese cuisine and one of the Four Great Traditions (四大菜系). It is derived from the native cooking style of Shandong Province, a northern coastal province of China."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"shandong-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shandong_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Bazirou","dish/bazirou"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/e/ec/Mianjin_hongshao_paigu_2009_03.jpg/500px-Mianjin_hongshao_paigu_2009_03.jpg","w":500,"h":301,"by":"Rolfmueller","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mianjin_hongshao_paigu_2009_03.jpg"}},"cuisine/nouvelle-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/nouvelle-cuisine","n":"Nouvelle cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in France","intro":[["Nouvelle cuisine is an approach to cooking and food presentation in French cuisine. In contrast to cuisine classique, an older form of haute cuisine, nouvelle cuisine is characterized by lighter, more delicate dishes and an increased emphasis on presentation. It was popularized in the 1960s by the food critic Henri Gault, who coined the phrase, and his colleagues André Gayot and Christian Millau in a new restaurant guide, the Gault Millau, or Le Nouveau Guide."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"nouvelle-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nouvelle_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Michel Guérard's Cuisine Minceur","ref":"cookbook/michel-guerard-s-cuisine-minceur","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Michel Guérard, 1976","note":"Lighter, sauce-reduced French cooking by one of the founders of nouvelle cuisine.","url":"","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/0/07/Jacques_Lameloise_DSCF6580.jpg/500px-Jacques_Lameloise_DSCF6580.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Jacques Lameloise - Edité par Arnaud 25","lic":"CC BY 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacques_Lameloise_DSCF6580.jpg"}},"cuisine/cuisine-of-monmouthshire":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/cuisine-of-monmouthshire","n":"Cuisine of Monmouthshire","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Welsh cuisine","cuisine/welsh-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in United Kingdom","intro":[["Monmouthshire has three main agricultural areas. The central and eastern part of the county is made up of the large valleys of the rivers Severn, Wye, and Usk and the land in this part of the county is fertile and of good quality with gently undulating land suitable for market gardens and vineyards. The southern part of the county is bounded by the Bristol Channel and includes areas of moorland and marsh, such as Wentlooge and Caldicot. This area was once owned by the monks of Goldcliff Priory who drained the land and built seawalls to prevent the sea flooding the land. The northern and western part of the county is made up of uplands with shallow soils of low nutrient value which are suitable for hill farming. This area includes the valleys of the rivers Ebbw and Sirhowy to the west and the Black Mountains to the north."],["Gilli Davies, the cook and author, notes that Monmouthshire has been successful in expanding the breadth of its food production into areas such as viticulture, horticulture and organic farming. In addition, food marketing initiatives have been created for specialist producers which have thrived, according to Davies, due to good transport links and the close proximity of the county to the markets of England. This helps in food distribution and in the development of a high-quality restaurant trade, with the latter having developed due to the efforts of some key individuals. Davies comments that there is \"a rare and appealing quality to the food in Monmouthshire\"."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"cuisine-of-monmouthshire"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuisine_of_Monmouthshire"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Welsh cuisine","cuisine/welsh-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Welsh cuisine","cuisine/welsh-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/d/d7/%E2%80%9CThe_First_Meeting_of_the_Hermit_and_the_Traveller%E2%80%9D%2C_from_the_book_%E2%80%9CThe_First_Principles_of_Good_Cookery_%E2%80%9Cby_Lady_Llanover%2C_first_published_in_1867.jpg/500px-%E2%80%9CThe_First_Meeting_of_the_Hermit_and_the_Traveller%E2%80%9D%2C_from_the_book_%E2%80%9CThe_First_Principles_of_Good_Cookery_%E2%80%9Cby_Lady_Llanover%2C_first_published_in_1867.jpg","w":500,"h":719,"by":"Lady Llanover","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E2%80%9CThe_First_Meeting_of_the_Hermit_and_the_Traveller%E2%80%9D%2C_from_the_book_%E2%80%9CThe_First_Principles_of_Good_Cookery_%E2%80%9Cby_Lady_Llanover%2C_first_published_in_1867.jpg"}},"ingredient/asparagus-bean":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/asparagus-bean","n":"Asparagus bean","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 4 dishes across 1 cuisine","intro":[["The asparagus bean (Vigna unguiculata subsp. sesquipedalis) is a legume cultivated for its edible green pods containing immature seeds, like the green bean. It is also known as yardlong bean, pea bean, long-podded cowpea, Chinese long bean, snake bean, bodi, bora, and kosbanti (kousenband). Despite the common name of \"yardlong\", the pods are actually only about half a yard long, so the subspecies name sesquipedalis (one-and-a-half-foot-long; 1.5 feet (0.50 yd)) is a more accurate approximation."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-asparagus-bean"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asparagus_bean"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Arsik","dish/arsik"],["Kapurung","dish/kapurung"],["Lawar (food)","dish/lawar-food"],["Pecel","dish/pecel"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Bay leaf","ingredient/bay-leaf"],["Candlenut","ingredient/candlenut"],["Chives","ingredient/chives"],["Etlingera elatior","ingredient/etlingera-elatior"],["Galangal","ingredient/galangal"],["Garcinia atroviridis","ingredient/garcinia-atroviridis"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Goldfish","ingredient/goldfish"],["Lemongrass","ingredient/lemongrass"],["Anchovy","ingredient/anchovy"],["Cayenne pepper","ingredient/cayenne-pepper"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Maize","ingredient/maize"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/5e/Vigna_unguiculata_Blanco2.286-cropped.jpg/500px-Vigna_unguiculata_Blanco2.286-cropped.jpg","w":500,"h":714,"by":"Francisco Manuel Blanco (O.S.A.)","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vigna_unguiculata_Blanco2.286-cropped.jpg"}},"ingredient/ras-el-hanout":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/ras-el-hanout","n":"Ras el hanout","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Ras el hanout or rass el hanout is a spice mix found in varying forms in Tunisia, Algeria, and Morocco. The name means 'head of the shop' in Arabic and implies a mixture of the best spices the seller has to offer. Ras el hanout is used in many savoury dishes, sometimes rubbed on meat or fish, or stirred into couscous or rice."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-ras-el-hanout"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ras_el_hanout"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Moroccan cuisine","cuisine/moroccan-cuisine",[["Babbouche","dish/babbouche"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Anise","ingredient/anise"],["Elettaria cardamomum","ingredient/elettaria-cardamomum"],["Liquorice","ingredient/liquorice"],["Mentha pulegium","ingredient/mentha-pulegium"],["Snail","ingredient/snail"],["Thymus","ingredient/thymus"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/a/a2/Ras.el.hanout.jpg/500px-Ras.el.hanout.jpg","w":500,"h":404,"by":"Sarefo","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ras.el.hanout.jpg"}},"dish/barbecue-sauce":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/barbecue-sauce","n":"Barbecue sauce","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from American cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Barbecue sauce (also abbreviated as BBQ sauce) is a sauce used as a marinade, basting, or topping for meat cooked in the barbecue cooking style, including pork, beef, and chicken. It is a ubiquitous condiment in the Southern United States and Australia which is used on many other foods as well."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-barbecue-sauce"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barbecue_sauce"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":5,"links":[["Ketchup","ingredient/ketchup"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Tomato paste","ingredient/tomato-paste"],["Tomato purée","ingredient/tomato-puree"],["Vinegar","ingredient/vinegar"]]},{"h":"More from American cuisine","links":[["Angel food cake","dish/angel-food-cake"],["Banana bread","dish/banana-bread"],["Banana pudding","dish/banana-pudding"],["Beer cake","dish/beer-cake"],["Better than sex cake","dish/better-than-sex-cake"],["Blini","dish/blini"],["Blooming onion","dish/blooming-onion"],["Brown Betty (dessert)","dish/brown-betty-dessert"],["Brunswick stew","dish/brunswick-stew"],["Buffalo wing","dish/buffalo-wing"],["Butter cake","dish/butter-cake"],["Caesar salad","dish/caesar-salad"],["Caramel apple","dish/caramel-apple"],["Carbonara","dish/carbonara"],["Cheesesteak","dish/cheesesteak"],["Chicago-style hot dog","dish/chicago-style-hot-dog"],["Chicken nugget","dish/chicken-nugget"],["Chiffon cake","dish/chiffon-cake"],["Chili con carne","dish/chili-con-carne"],["Chimichanga","dish/chimichanga"],["Chow-chow (food)","dish/chow-chow-food"],["Chowder","dish/chowder"],["Cioppino","dish/cioppino"],["Clam chowder",